{
  "is_sufficient": false,
  "knowledge_gaps": [
    "Prefix extraction and user attribution are underspecified. The loader provides no user IDs, yet aggregation requires them. Each positive must be paired with its corresponding causal prefix, excluding later sequence positions, with explicit feature selection and candidate-scoring tensor shapes.",
    "Exact-item recurrence may have little coverage after preprocessing and does not measure repeated interests across different items. Define repetition eligibility, grouping rules, removal counts, and handling of empty histories before interpreting results.",
    "Recent-event removal cannot generally have distinct controls matched on the same positions. Specify the comparison being estimated, matching tolerances, overlap with targeted removals, and how positional changes and unmatched cases affect interpretation.",
    "Diagnostic examples, intervention sizes, control draws, seeds, runtime caps, and decision rules for subsequent training ablations are unspecified. The proposal supplies useful exploratory measurements but does not yet demonstrate improved recommendation quality. Potential 8/10 is optimistic without that evidence, and difficulty 3/10 understates reliable implementation."
  ],
  "follow_up_queries": [
    {
      "reason": "Establish correct causal prefix construction and architecture-compatible scoring; user attribution also requires clarification of the local adapter.",
      "query": "In SASRec implementations with sequence, positive, and negative tensors shaped batch-by-length and shifted next-item targets, how should per-position training prefixes be extracted and independently scored for perturbation attribution without including future interactions? Examine causal masks, right-aligned positional embeddings, final valid feature selection, and preserving user identity for user-level aggregation."
    },
    {
      "reason": "Determine whether repetition probes are feasible and whether matched controls can distinguish evidence dependence from positional artifacts.",
      "query": "For sequential recommendation perturbation explanations on Amazon interaction datasets, how do preprocessing and repeated user-item deduplication affect exact-item recurrence coverage? What evaluation methods compare recent-history or repeated-interest removal against controls while accounting for absolute position changes, overlapping removals, and out-of-distribution shortened histories?"
    },
    {
      "reason": "Connect exploratory diagnosis to a reproducible improvement experiment while limiting adaptive overfitting to the fixed validation sample.",
      "query": "What research evaluates whether perturbation-based explanations in sequential recommendation predict successful model ablations? Investigate fixed diagnostic panels, paired intervention controls, matched training budgets, and adaptive validation overfitting when repeatedly selecting models using sampled HR@10 with 99 negatives and only 200 validation users per dataset."
    }
  ]
}
